DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The  functions described on this page are used for performing "nonlocal
       gotos": transferring execution from one function to a predetermined lo‐
       cation in another function.  The sseettjjmmpp() function  dynamically  estab‐
       lishes  the  target  to  which  control  will later be transferred, and
       lloonnggjjmmpp() performs the transfer of execution.

       The sseettjjmmpp() function saves various information about the calling envi‐
       ronment (typically, the stack pointer, the instruction pointer,  possi‐
       bly  the  values  of other registers and the signal mask) in the buffer
       _e_n_v for later use by lloonnggjjmmpp().  In this case, sseettjjmmpp() returns 0.

       The lloonnggjjmmpp() function uses the information saved in  _e_n_v  to  transfer
       control  back  to  the  point  where sseettjjmmpp() was called and to restore
       ("rewind") the stack to its state at the time of the sseettjjmmpp() call.  In
       addition, and depending on the implementation (see NOTES and  HISTORY),
       the  values  of some other registers and the process signal mask may be
       restored to their state at the time of the sseettjjmmpp() call.

       Following a successful lloonnggjjmmpp(), execution continues  as  if  sseettjjmmpp()
       had  returned  for  a  second  time.  This "fake" return can be distin‐
       guished from a true sseettjjmmpp() call because the "fake" return returns the
       value provided in _v_a_l.  If the programmer mistakenly passes the value 0
       in _v_a_l, the "fake" return will instead return 1.

   ssiiggsseettjjmmpp(()) aanndd ssiigglloonnggjjmmpp(())
       ssiiggsseettjjmmpp() and ssiigglloonnggjjmmpp() also perform nonlocal gotos,  but  provide
       predictable handling of the process signal mask.

       If,  and  only  if,  the  _s_a_v_e_s_i_g_s  argument provided to ssiiggsseettjjmmpp() is
       nonzero, the process’s current signal mask is saved in _e_n_v and will  be
       restored if a ssiigglloonnggjjmmpp() is later performed with this _e_n_v.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEE
       sseettjjmmpp()  and  ssiiggsseettjjmmpp() return 0 when called directly; on the "fake"
       return that occurs after lloonnggjjmmpp() or ssiigglloonnggjjmmpp(), the  nonzero  value
       specified in _v_a_l is returned.

       The lloonnggjjmmpp() or ssiigglloonnggjjmmpp() functions do not return.

