       POSIX does not specify whether sseettjjmmpp() will save the signal  mask  (to
       be  later  restored  during  lloonnggjjmmpp()).   In System V it will not.  In
       4.3BSD it will, and there is a function __sseettjjmmpp() that will  not.   The
       behavior  under  Linux  depends on the glibc version and the setting of
       feature test macros.  Before glibc 2.19, sseettjjmmpp() follows the System  V
       behavior   by  default,  but  the  BSD  behavior  is  provided  if  the
       __BBSSDD__SSOOUURRCCEE feature test  macro  is  explicitly  defined  and  none  of
       __PPOOSSIIXX__SSOOUURRCCEE,    __PPOOSSIIXX__CC__SSOOUURRCCEE,   __XXOOPPEENN__SSOOUURRCCEE,   __GGNNUU__SSOOUURRCCEE,   or
       __SSVVIIDD__SSOOUURRCCEE is defined.  Since glibc 2.19, _<_s_e_t_j_m_p_._h_> exposes only the
       System V version of sseettjjmmpp().  Programs that  need  the  BSD  semantics
       should  replace  calls  to  sseettjjmmpp()  with  calls to ssiiggsseettjjmmpp() with a
       nonzero _s_a_v_e_s_i_g_s argument.

NNOOTTEESS
       sseettjjmmpp() and lloonnggjjmmpp() can be useful for  dealing  with  errors  inside
       deeply  nested  function  calls  or  to  allow a signal handler to pass
       control to a specific point in the program, rather  than  returning  to
       the  point  where  the  handler  interrupted  the main program.  In the
       latter case, if you want to portably save and restore signal masks, use
       ssiiggsseettjjmmpp() and ssiigglloonnggjjmmpp().   See  also  the  discussion  of  program
       readability below.

CCAAVVEEAATTSS
       The  compiler  may optimize variables into registers, and lloonnggjjmmpp() may
       restore the values of other registers in addition to the stack  pointer
       and  program  counter.  Consequently, the values of automatic variables
       are unspecified after  a  call  to  lloonnggjjmmpp()  if  they  meet  all  the
       following criteria:

       •  they  are local to the function that made the corresponding sseettjjmmpp()
          call;

       •  their  values  are  changed  between  the  calls  to  sseettjjmmpp()   and
          lloonnggjjmmpp(); and

       •  they are not declared as _v_o_l_a_t_i_l_e.

       Analogous remarks apply for ssiigglloonnggjjmmpp().

   NNoonnllooccaall ggoottooss aanndd pprrooggrraamm rreeaaddaabbiilliittyy
       While it can be abused, the traditional C "goto" statement at least has
       the benefit that lexical cues (the goto statement and the target label)
       allow  the programmer to easily perceive the flow of control.  Nonlocal
       gotos provide no such cues: multiple sseettjjmmpp() calls  might  employ  the
       same  _j_m_p___b_u_f  variable  so that the content of the variable may change
       over the lifetime of the application.  Consequently, the programmer may
       be forced to perform detailed reading of  the  code  to  determine  the
       dynamic   target   of  a  particular  lloonnggjjmmpp()  call.   (To  make  the
       programmer’s life easier, each sseettjjmmpp() call  should  employ  a  unique
       _j_m_p___b_u_f variable.)

       Adding  further  difficulty,  the  sseettjjmmpp() and lloonnggjjmmpp() calls may not
       even be in the same source code module.

       In summary, nonlocal gotos can make programs harder to  understand  and
       maintain, and an alternative should be used if possible.

   UUnnddeeffiinneedd bbeehhaavviioorr
       If  the  function  which  called  sseettjjmmpp()  returns before lloonnggjjmmpp() is
       called, the behavior is undefined.  Some kind  of  subtle  or  unsubtle
       chaos is sure to result.

       If,  in a multithreaded program, a lloonnggjjmmpp() call employs an _e_n_v buffer
       that was initialized by a call to sseettjjmmpp() in a different  thread,  the
       behavior is undefined.

       POSIX.1‐2008 Technical Corrigendum 2 adds lloonnggjjmmpp() and ssiigglloonnggjjmmpp() to
       the   list  of  async‐signal‐safe  functions.   However,  the  standard
       recommends avoiding the use of these functions from signal handlers and
       goes on to point out that if these functions are called from  a  signal
       handler that interrupted a call to a non‐async‐signal‐safe function (or
       some  equivalent,  such  as  the steps equivalent to eexxiitt(3) that occur
       upon a return from  the  initial  call  to  _m_a_i_n()),  the  behavior  is
       undefined  if  the  program  subsequently  makes a call to a non‐async‐
       signal‐safe function.  The only way of avoiding undefined  behavior  is
       to ensure one of the following:

       •  After  long  jumping  from  the signal handler, the program does not
          call any non‐async‐signal‐safe functions and does  not  return  from
          the initial call to _m_a_i_n().

       •  Any signal whose handler performs a long jump must be blocked during
          _e_v_e_r_y  call  to  a  non‐async‐signal‐safe function and no non‐async‐
          signal‐safe functions are called after returning  from  the  initial
          call to _m_a_i_n().

